WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.800 --> 00:00:04.370
- Do you think for teaching and in general

00:00:04.370 --> 00:00:05.710
thinking about new concepts,

00:00:05.710 --> 00:00:08.583
do you think it's better
to plug in the numbers,

00:00:09.850 --> 00:00:12.060
or to think more abstractly?

00:00:12.060 --> 00:00:16.610
So, looking at theorems
and proving the theorems

00:00:16.610 --> 00:00:21.220
or actually building up a
basic intuition of the theorem

00:00:21.220 --> 00:00:22.900
or the method, the approach,

00:00:22.900 --> 00:00:26.320
and then just plugging in
numbers and seeing it work?

00:00:26.320 --> 00:00:31.320
- Well, certainly many of
us like to see examples.

00:00:32.341 --> 00:00:34.020
We understand.

00:00:34.020 --> 00:00:36.910
It might be a pretty
abstract sounding example

00:00:36.910 --> 00:00:39.760
like a three dimensional rotation.

00:00:39.760 --> 00:00:44.213
How are you gonna
understand a rotation in 3D?

00:00:45.851 --> 00:00:48.657
Or in 10D?

00:00:50.860 --> 00:00:53.800
And then some of us like
to keep going with it

00:00:53.800 --> 00:00:55.690
to the point where you got numbers.

00:00:55.690 --> 00:01:00.073
Where you got 10 angles,
10 axes, 10 angles.

00:01:04.785 --> 00:01:06.590
The great mathematicians probably,

00:01:06.590 --> 00:01:07.720
I don't know if they do that,

00:01:07.720 --> 00:01:12.720
'cause for them an example would be

00:01:15.180 --> 00:01:17.650
a highly abstract thing to the rest of us.

00:01:17.650 --> 00:01:18.483
- Right.

00:01:18.483 --> 00:01:20.510
But nevertheless, working
with in the space of examples.

00:01:20.510 --> 00:01:21.370
- Yeah, examples.

00:01:21.370 --> 00:01:22.210
- It seems to.

00:01:22.210 --> 00:01:24.800
- Examples of structure.

00:01:24.800 --> 00:01:26.630
- Our brains seem to connect with that.

00:01:26.630 --> 00:01:27.463
- Yeah, yeah.

